# DURABLE REPRO — `E_BorrowAcrossAwait` appears to be a DEAD GUARD, and it is
# the SECOND of a propping-each-other-up PAIR.
#
# `docs/language-reference.md` §7.24 documents a borrow held across an `await`
# as an error. At HEAD this program is ACCEPTED: `gg check` reports "OK: no
# semantic errors".
#
# WRONG TODAY (measured at HEAD, Rust gg). Shapes probed, all accepted:
#   `&`-param held across await   (this fixture)
#   collection-element view held across await
#     (`auto r = v.get(0).unwrap()` ... `await` ... `print(r.x)`)
#
# ⚠ NO POSITIVE CONTROL (Core #13). Every reference to
# `SemanticErrorKind::BorrowAcrossAwait` in `src/semantic/safety/tests.rs` is a
# NEGATIVE assertion (`!has_error`) — not one test asserts it FIRES.
#
# MECHANISM. There is exactly ONE raise site (`safety/origins.rs:81`), gated on
# `self.await_invalidated.contains(&def_id)`. That set has exactly ONE insert
# (`safety/check_expr.rs:853`), reached only for a variable whose
# `BorrowOrigin::contains_local()` holds. Since the StringView removal made
# String params and locals OWNED rather than borrowed, the shapes that used to
# populate it appear to have stopped existing.
#
# ⚠⚠ THE PAIR. The filter that gates that insert carries this comment
# (`check_expr.rs:838-840`):
#     "This is sound because Change 1 (Spawn enforcement) prevents
#      borrowed refs from reaching fire-and-forget spawns."
# That spawn enforcement is `E_SpawnWithBorrowedRef`, which has ALSO been
# measured to have no positive control and no reachable shape — see the sibling
# repro `sound_spawn_borrowed_ref_never_rejected.gg`. So the async-borrow guard
# justifies its narrowness by pointing at the spawn guard, and neither one has
# been demonstrated to fire. Investigate them TOGETHER; a fix or a deletion of
# either changes the argument for the other. This is a Core #14 case squared —
# an invariant-asserting comment whose named enforcer is itself unenforced.
#
# INTENDED: one of two, and the round that closes this must pick — the SAME
# fork as the spawn sibling.
#   (a) The rule is live and the reachable shape was not found → this fixture
#       graduates to a NEG asserting the rejection, plus a positive-control
#       unit test.
#   (b) The rule is obsolete because the borrow model changed under it → DELETE
#       the raise site, the variant, and `await_invalidated`, and rewrite §7.24
#       to describe what actually protects a value across a suspension.
# A rule documented in the reference with nothing enforcing it is a Core #14
# defect either way.
#
# ⚠ THIS FIXTURE ASSERTS NEITHER OUTCOME. It records that the documented
# rejection does not happen. Do not read its acceptance as a pin on the accept
# side.
#
# ORACLE (ggdef): OUT OF SUBSET — ggdef has no `async`/`await`. Recorded per
# Core #9.
#
# The seed is COMPUTED, not a literal, so no const-fold can elide it.
#
# Cited from TODO.md (safety / async / Medium).

struct Point:
    int x

int seed():
    return 7

async int some_task():
    return 1

async void process_ref(Point &p):
    some_task().await()
    print(p.x)

async void main():
    Point p = Point(seed())
    await process_ref(&p)
